      intro: "Iʿjāz ʿIlmī literally combines two words: iʿjāz (\"that which renders an opponent incapable of matching or responding to it\") and ʿilmī (\"of knowledge/science\"). It describes the specific claim that the Qurʾān and Sunnah contain information about the natural world that could not have been known in 7th-century Arabia and that later matches modern discovery. This book documents that claim with its real, named sources — while being honest that this is a specific, contested modern interpretive tradition, not a single settled ancient consensus.<div class='xn-diagram-wrap'><div class='xn-diagram-title' style='color:var(--burgundy)'>How Every Topic in This Book Is Built (tap each stage)</div><div class='xn-flow-wrap'><div class='xn-flow-row'><details class='xn-flow-node'><summary>📜 A Text</summary><div class='xn-flow-node-body'>A specific verse or ḥadīth, quoted in Arabic and translation, never paraphrased away from its actual wording.</div></details><span class='xn-flow-arrow'>→</span><details class='xn-flow-node'><summary>🔬 A Named Finding</summary><div class='xn-flow-node-body'>A specific modern scientist, study, or textbook — cited with author, year, and page where available — read as corroborating that wording.</div></details><span class='xn-flow-arrow'>→</span><details class='xn-flow-node'><summary>⚖️ An Honest Caveat</summary><div class='xn-flow-node-body'>Where a claim is genuinely contested — by classical exegesis, by scientists, or by the specific conditions under which a testimonial was given — this book says so explicitly, in the same section.</div></details></div></div></div>",
