      intro: 'Two verses describe mountains as "pegs" (awtād) and as stabilizers against the earth "shifting" — read against the 19th- and 20th-century discovery that mountains have deep roots and play a genuine role in crustal stability.<div class="xn-diagram-wrap"><div class="xn-diagram-title" style="color:var(--burgundy-light)">A Peg, Cut Away (tap to see the two halves)</div><div class="xn-flow-wrap"><div class="xn-flow-row"><details class="xn-flow-node"><summary>Above the surface</summary><div class="xn-flow-node-body">The visible peak — a small fraction of the structure, the only part a 7th-century observer could see.</div></details><span class="xn-flow-arrow">+</span><details class="xn-flow-node"><summary>Below the surface</summary><div class="xn-flow-node-body">A root reaching several times the peak’s height (Press, Earth, p.435) — matching a watad (peg), whose defining feature is that most of it is buried.</div></details><span class="xn-flow-arrow">=</span><details class="xn-flow-node"><summary>The crustal-stability role</summary><div class="xn-flow-node-body">Plate tectonics (late 1960s onward) treats this root structure as helping stabilize the crust against shifting — Q16:15’s specific claim.</div></details></div></div></div>',
